NNAAMMEE
     aarrpp — address resolution display and control

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aarrpp [--aaddnn] [--VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n] _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
     aarrpp [--FF] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n] --ss _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp | ppeerrmmaanneenntt]
         [ppuubb]
     aarrpp --WW _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [_i_f_a_c_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aarrpp program displays and modifies the Internet-to-Ethernet address
     translation tables used by the address resolution protocol (ARP).

     aarrpp displays the current ARP entry for _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e when no optional parame‐
     ters are supplied.  _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e may be specified by name or by number, using
     Internet dot notation.

     aarrpp can also be used to send Wake on LAN (WoL) frames over a local Ether‐
     net network to one or more hosts using their link layer (hardware) ad‐
     dresses.  WoL functionality is generally enabled in a machine's BIOS and
     can be used to power on machines from a remote system without having
     physical access to them.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa      Display all of the current ARP entries.  See also the --dd option
             below.  The following information will be printed:

                Host    The network address of the host.

                Ethernet Address
                        The Ethernet address of the host.  If the address is
                        not available, it will be displayed as “(incomplete)”.

                Netif   The network interface associated with the ARP entry.

                Expire  The time until expiry of the entry.  If the entry is
                        marked “permanent” or “static”, it will never expire.

                Flags   Flags on the ARP entry, in a single letter.  They are:
                        local (‘l’) and published (‘p’).

     --dd      Delete an entry for the host called _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e.  Alternatively, the
             --dd flag may be combined with the --aa flag to delete all entries,
             with hostname lookups automatically disabled.  Only the superuser
             may delete entries.

     --FF      Force existing entries for the given host to be overwritten (only
             relevant to the --ff and --ss options).

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Process entries from _f_i_l_e to be set in the ARP tables.  See the
             --ss option for a description of the file format and the effect of
             the --FF option.

     --nn      Do not perform domain name resolution.  If a name cannot be re‐
             solved without DNS, an error will be reported.

     --ss _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp | ppeerrmmaanneenntt] [ppuubb]
             Create an ARP entry for the host called _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e with the Ether‐
             net address _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r.  The Ethernet address is given as six
             hexadecimal bytes separated by colons.  The entry will be static
             (will not time out) unless the word tteemmpp is given in the command.
             A static ARP entry can be overwritten by network traffic, unless
             the word ppeerrmmaanneenntt is given.  If the word ppuubb is given, the entry
             will be “published”; that is, this system will act as an ARP
             server, responding to requests for _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e even though the host
             address is not its own.  This behavior has traditionally been
             called _p_r_o_x_y _A_R_P.

             If the entry already exists for the given host, it will not be
             replaced unless --FF is given.

     --VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n
             Select the routing domain.

     --WW _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [_i_f_a_c_e]
             Send the Wake on LAN frame from all interfaces on the local ma‐
             chine that are up, if _i_f_a_c_e has not been specified.  Otherwise
             the frame will be sent from _i_f_a_c_e.  _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r is the Ethernet
             address of the remote machine or a hostname entry in _/_e_t_c_/_e_t_h_e_r_s.
             This option cannot be used in combination with any other option.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/ethers  Ethernet host name database.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     View the current arp(4) table, showing network addresses symbolically:

           $ arp -a

     Create a permanent entry (one that cannot be overwritten by other network
     traffic):

           # arp -s 10.0.0.2 00:90:27:bb:cc:dd permanent

     Create proxy ARP entries on interface fxp0 (MAC address
     00:90:27:bb:cc:dd), for IP addresses 204.1.2.3 and 204.1.2.4:

           # arp -s 204.1.2.3 00:90:27:bb:cc:dd pub
           # arp -s 204.1.2.4 00:90:27:bb:cc:dd pub

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     inet_addr(3), arp(4), ethers(5), ifconfig(8), ndp(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The aarrpp command appeared in 4.3BSD.  Wake on LAN functionality was added
     in OpenBSD 4.9.

